{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"duration_ms":65445,"duration_api_ms":54779,"num_turns":8,"result":"After adjusting for pre-2020 firm characteristics, more remote work is associated with more patenting, which fits your expectation. It is an observational association, not proof of cause.\n\n**Preferred estimate:** a 1-unit increase in `remote_share` goes with 0.75 more patents per 100 engineers in 2023 (SE 0.233, p = 0.0013, 95% CI about 0.29 to 1.21). `remote_share` is standardized, so one unit is one standard deviation. The outcome averages about 100, so the effect is modest. The model is a regression of 2023 patents per 100 engineers on `remote_share`, controlling for all pre-period firm characteristics (including 2020 patents), with robust standard errors across 2000 firms.\n\n**Other specifications:**\n- **Raw model:** The unadjusted association is 2.32, about three times larger. It is confounded by firm size, age and similar traits that relate to both remote work and patenting.\n- **Stability:** Three adjusted variants give 0.75 to 0.83. The consistency doesn't rule out unobserved confounders.\n- **Collaboration index:** Adding `collab_index_2023` flips the sign to −2.13. I excluded it because it is measured in 2023, after the remote work period, and remote work strongly raises it (coefficient 0.68, p ≈ 6e-129). Controlling for it is not a valid causal comparison.\n\nI estimated 6 models in total, and the report lists all of them.\n\nFiles are in the working folder:\n- report.md\n- final_answer.json","session_id":"cd3d4bcf-3edd-40de-8a93-c86ad3f2ef06","total_cost_usd":0.0908326,"usage":{"input_tokens":14,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6194,"cache_read_input_tokens":48427,"output_tokens":2984,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6194}},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":3227,"outputTokens":